  "query_preview": "..Welcome to the halfway mark of the 2026 Monsoon, \nwhich, depending on where one lives in southeast Arizona, either\nhas been poor so far (northern Graham, parts of SE Pinal, and \nparts of Tucson metro) or very good, (Cochise and Santa Cruz \ncounties and eastern parts of the Tohono O'odham nation).\n\nSatellite imagery early this morning showed a slowly decaying \nMCS across northeast Sonora MX, while radar showed a a couple \nelevated light showers between Nogales and Sierra Vista. Current\ncloud cover over the area will slowly decrease this morning. \nFor today, streamlines shows weak disturbance at 500 mb while \nat 300 mb there will be a slight increase in upper level \ndivergence. Storm activity over the mountains will be slow to \nget going, with scattered storms developing after 2 pm and \nslowly moving off the higher terrain under NE flow aloft. Main \nthreat from storms will be strong gusty winds with 07/00z HREF \nshowing neighborhood probabilities of 10% for 58+ mph wind gusts\nmainly across the eastern zones with Tucson on the western \nperiphery. Can't run out localized heavy rain and flash \nflooding, especially along the Intl border.\n7\nThis weekend into next week, expect daily chances of showers and\nthunderstorms with day-to-day variability in areal coverage\nacross SE Arizona. The usual monsoon hazards will be around \neach day. \n\nHigh temperatures today through Sunday will run 5 to 8 degrees\nabove normal, then be around normal next week.\n\n8 to 14 day outlooks (August 14-20):\nBoth temperature and precipitation are leaning above normal.",
